**user**:
> How do people in the EU write 3pm? I feel like sometimes I see 3pm and sometimes 1500 or 15:00 or 15hr.
**assistant**:
> There’s no single EU convention, but **24-hour time is much more common** than in the US.
> 
> - **15:00** — broadly standard and safest for international/EU-facing writing.
> - **15.00** — common in some countries, especially Germany, Denmark, Italy, and parts of Central/Northern Europe.
> - **15h / 15 h 00** — common in French-language contexts (France, Belgium, Luxembourg).
> - **1500** — used in military, aviation, some schedules, and terse professional contexts; less natural in ordinary prose.
> - **3 pm / 3 p.m.** — especially common in the UK and Ireland, and understood everywhere, but less typical on continental Europe.
> - **15hr** — not a standard way to write a clock time. “15 hr” usually suggests a duration (“a 15-hour trip”).
> 
> For a Europe-wide audience, write **15:00** (and include the time zone when relevant, e.g. **15:00 CEST**).
**user**:
> What about 3 - 5pm?
**assistant**:
> For an EU-wide audience: **15:00–17:00**.
> 
> Also common:
> - **15.00–17.00** in countries using periods.
> - **15h–17h** in French contexts.
> - **3–5 pm** is natural in the UK/Ireland, but less standard across continental Europe.
> 
> Use an en dash without spaces: **15:00–17:00**.
